=== layout: one-panel

# Terminology for Cross Experiments

@body

- P\: parental generation
- F1\: offspring of P
- F2\: offspring of F1
- F3\: offspring of F2
- F4\: ...

=== layout: two-panels

# True Breeding

@left

- Garden peas allow for self-pollination as well.
  - Mendel was able to determine if a plant was true-breeding by self-pollinating the plant and looking at its offspring.
- True-breeding plants always will produce offspring of the same phenotype.

@right

![True-breeding purple AA plants produce purple AA offspring in each generation](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/pea_true_breeding.drawn.svg)

=== layout: two-panels

# Hybrid Plants

@left

- In order to study traits, Mendel conducted cross experiments.
  - Garden peas allow for self-pollination as well.
- Mendel produced hybrid plants using controlled cross-pollination of true-breeding parents
  - Crossing true-breeding plants that differ in a  trait produces a hybrid.

@right

![AA purple and aa white parents produce Aa purple hybrids; the next generation includes purple and white plants](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/pea_hybrid_plants.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Zygosity Vocabulary

@body

- An organism with two identical alleles for a character is homozygous for the gene controlling that character
  - true-breeding organisms are homozygous
- An organism that has two different alleles for a gene is heterozygous for the gene controlling that character
  - heterozygotes are hybrids and not true-breeding

=== layout: one-panel

# The Monohybrid Cross

@body

- A monohybrid cross is a mating between individuals who have different alleles at one genetic locus of interest.
- A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for one character is called a monohybrid cross

=== layout: one-panel

# Cross Experiment: P Generation

@body

![True-breeding purple-flowered and white-flowered parents](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/pea_experiment_p.drawn.svg)

- Cross true-breeding purple-flowered and white-flowered plants.
- Predict: What flower color will their offspring have?

=== layout: one-panel

# Cross Experiment: F1 Generation

@body

![The parental cross produces F1 hybrids with purple flowers](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/pea_experiment_f1.drawn.svg)

- All F1 plants had purple flowers.
- Allow self-pollination or cross-pollination between F1 plants.
- Predict: What flower colors will appear in the F2 generation?

=== layout: one-panel

# Cross Experiment: F2 Generation

@body

![Self- or cross-pollination of F1 plants produces purple and white F2 plants](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/pea_experiment_f2.drawn.svg)

- Observed F2 offspring: [*705 purple-flowered plants*]{color=purple} and *224 white-flowered plants*.
- The white-flower phenotype reappeared after self- or cross-pollination of the F1 hybrids.

=== layout: two-panels

# Hybrid Cross Terminology

@left

- *P*: parental generation; true-breeding purple and white parents, homozygous for flower color.
- *F1*: offspring of P; heterozygous hybrids, all with purple flowers.
- *F2*: offspring of F1 after self- or cross-pollination.
  - Observed: 705 purple-flowered and 224 white-flowered plants, approximately 3\:1.
- *F3*: offspring of F2; *F4*: offspring of F3, and so on.

@right

![P parents produce purple F1 hybrids; F1 self- or cross-pollination produces purple and white F2 offspring](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/pea_cross_recap.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Hybrid Crosses

@body

- A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for [one character]{color=red} is called a [monohybrid]{color=red} cross
  - [Aa &times; Aa]{color=red}
- A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for two characters is called a dihybrid cross
  - AaBb &times; AaBb
- A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for [three characters]{color=green} is called a [trihybrid ]{color=green}cross
  - [AaBbCc &times; AaBbCc]{color=green}

=== layout: section

# Reciprocal Crosses

=== layout: one-panel

# Reciprocal Crosses

@body

![Slide image 7](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/image_007.png)

- Pea plants are sexual organisms
- A cross can be preformed in two ways\:
  - Male purple x Female [white]{color=gray}
  - Male [white]{color=gray} x Female purple
- For Mendel, the outcome of reciprocal cross did not depend on which trait was in the male or the female\:
- A reciprocal cross should show no difference, unless the gene is sex-linked (Chapter 5)

=== layout: section

# Testcross

=== layout: two-panels

# Testcross

@left

- A cross between a heterozygote (or unknown) and a [homozygous recessive]{color=red} is a testcross
- In a testcross of a Ww heterozygous parent with a ww homozygous recessive, the progeny are Ww and ww in the ratio of 1 \: 1.

@right

![Slide image 8](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/sheep_testcross.drawn.svg)

=== layout: two-panels

# Testcross

@left

- The phenotypes of the progeny reveal the relative frequencies of the different gametes produced by the heterozygous parent
- The recessive parent contributes only recessive alleles.

@right

![Slide image 9](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/pea_testcross.drawn.svg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Mendel's testcrosses

@body

![Slide image 10](assets/lect04d-cross_experiments/image_010.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel

# Types of Crosses

@body

- Testcross\: breeding the mystery individual with a homozygous recessive individual
- Hybrid crosses\:
  - A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for one character is called a monohybrid cross
  - A cross between two individuals that are both heterozygous for two characters is called a dihybrid cross
  - Three characters is a trihybrid cross, etc.
- Backcross\: hybrid organisms (F1) are crossed with one of the parental (P) genotypes
- Reciprocal cross\: swapping the traits of the male and female in a cross.

